1
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:22,760
One of the critical issues of the present nuclear programs in the United States

2
00:00:22,860 --> 00:00:27,859
is the long-term disposal of nuclear waste from commercial reactors.

3
00:00:27,959 --> 00:00:44,560
To solve this problem, the U.S. Department of Energy is presently undertaking a number of programs throughout the country to address the possibility of geologic storage in various formations.

4
00:00:45,639 --> 00:00:56,739
The United States Department of Energy is examining the feasibility of different sites for repositories in various rock formations such as granite, shale, and basalt.

5
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:01,939
Salt is one of the rock types being considered.

6
00:01:02,039 --> 00:01:05,359
Bedded and domed salt deposits are found throughout the US,

7
00:01:05,459 --> 00:01:08,980
especially in the Northeast and Gulf Coast states.

8
00:01:09,080 --> 00:01:14,420
The Office of Nuclear Waste Isolation of Battelle Memorial Institute is managing these studies.

9
00:01:15,839 --> 00:01:19,260
Research in granite, volcanic tufts, and shale

10
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:25,239
is being performed by the Department of Energy and Sandia Laboratories at the Nevada nuclear test site

11
00:01:25,339 --> 00:01:27,640
where these rock types occur.

12
00:01:27,740 --> 00:01:32,260
These rocks are also found in other parts of the United States.

13
00:01:32,360 --> 00:01:37,859
The basalt effort is concentrated on the Columbia Plateau of southeastern Washington with emphasis

14
00:01:37,959 --> 00:01:43,219
on the Pasco Basin where the Hanford site of the U.S. Department of Energy is located.

15
00:01:43,319 --> 00:01:47,519
These studies are being conducted by Rockwell Hanford Operations.

16
00:01:47,619 --> 00:01:52,879
A great deal is known about the Columbia Plateau basalts because state and federal agencies

17
00:01:52,979 --> 00:01:56,700
and academic institutions have studied the basalts in detail.

18
00:01:56,800 --> 00:02:06,359
Drilling and mapping efforts have taken place related to irrigation projects, hydroelectric dams, nuclear reactors, and nuclear reprocessing plants at the Hanford site.

19
00:02:07,179 --> 00:02:17,039
Present basalt studies involved a large scientific staff here at Rockwell Hanford, as well as numerous other contractors throughout the United States.

20
00:02:17,139 --> 00:02:42,319
As part of these studies, we are attempting to look at the geologic characteristics of this most important rock that we call basalt, the fracture patterns, various properties of this rock, the hydrologic properties of the terrain in which this rock exists, the waste-basalt interactions, the interactions between this rock and the waste in place.

21
00:02:42,419 --> 00:02:49,819
in it, the engineering properties including the strength of this material, and eventually

22
00:02:49,919 --> 00:02:56,599
demonstrating whether or not it is feasible to site a repository in this type of formation.

23
00:02:56,699 --> 00:03:01,400
Geologic studies in the area require the availability of rock samples.

24
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:05,719
Thousands of feet of basalt core have been obtained through drilling activity.

25
00:03:05,819 --> 00:03:09,919
These cores are then studied by scientists to determine the basic rock properties of

26
00:03:10,019 --> 00:03:11,479
the basalt.

27
00:03:11,580 --> 00:03:17,020
than 70,000 feet of vertical bore holes have been drilled within the Pasco Basin as part

28
00:03:17,120 --> 00:03:22,680
of the basalt studies. Cores and samples from these holes are stored in a warehouse within

29
00:03:22,780 --> 00:03:27,620
the Hanford site where the samples are preserved so they can be used for studies under strict

30
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:34,040
quality assurance guidelines. Geologic studies have required many field activities in addition

31
00:03:34,140 --> 00:03:40,820
to drilling. Mapping of observable features, outcrops, water gaps, and roadcuts have been

32
00:03:40,919 --> 00:03:45,139
conducted throughout the plateau. These studies supplement previous efforts by

33
00:03:45,239 --> 00:03:49,039
many geologists who have conducted studies of the basalt as part of other

34
00:03:49,139 --> 00:03:54,680
projects. Dr. Sue Price is studying the structure and characteristics of the

35
00:03:54,780 --> 00:03:59,840
basalts. Dr. Price and her colleagues spent months mapping many features and

36
00:03:59,940 --> 00:04:04,280
charting the stratigraphy of the area. The stratigraphy of the area is simply

37
00:04:04,380 --> 00:04:08,180
the compilation and analysis of all the layers that make up the subsurface

38
00:04:08,280 --> 00:04:14,280
geology. The basalts underlying the plateau include numerous individual flows ranging in thickness

39
00:04:14,380 --> 00:04:23,180
from a few feet to several hundred feet. The latter are best for repository siting. The high cliff

40
00:04:23,280 --> 00:04:29,540
behind me is comprised of several thick basalt layers called flows. Stratigraphic studies have

41
00:04:29,640 --> 00:04:35,579
been conducted to determine properties which may be used to distinguish one flow from another. Using

42
00:04:35,679 --> 00:04:41,079
stratigraphic techniques, geologists are not only able to identify certain flows exposed to the

43
00:04:41,179 --> 00:04:47,479
surface, but to identify these same flows were penetrated adept by drill holes. The development

44
00:04:47,579 --> 00:04:53,539
of stratigraphic techniques has enabled geologists to select thick and widespread flows such as the

45
00:04:53,639 --> 00:04:58,919
umptanum, which may be suitable locations for a deep basalt cavern within the Pasco Basin.

46
00:04:59,020 --> 00:05:06,060
Dr. Aaron Waters, a noted authority in basalt geology, is a member of a peer review group

47
00:05:06,160 --> 00:05:09,540
overseeing the basalt geologic studies.

48
00:05:09,640 --> 00:05:15,160
At Sentinel Gap, northeast of the Hanford site, he describes a typical basalt flow.

49
00:05:15,260 --> 00:05:22,639
These big columns are made as the lava cools.

50
00:05:22,739 --> 00:05:29,899
Here it is cooling from the base and rather slowly, and so the columns are large.

51
00:05:29,999 --> 00:05:36,879
The entablature above with the finer joints has cooled from the top, where heat is carried

52
00:05:36,979 --> 00:05:43,199
off more rapidly by convection into the air."

53
00:05:43,299 --> 00:05:48,300
Infrared imagery and other specialized technologies are being used to complement and speed up

54
00:05:48,400 --> 00:05:54,660
our geologic mapping of the 200,000 cubic kilometers covered by the Columbia Plateau.

55
00:05:54,760 --> 00:05:59,740
This work is being done to develop sufficient information to identify target horizons for

56
00:05:59,840 --> 00:06:04,480
potential repository sighting.

57
00:06:04,580 --> 00:06:10,160
To characterize the Hanford site, subsurface definition and analysis of geologic structures

58
00:06:10,260 --> 00:06:15,060
have been accomplished by three different geophysical seismic systems.

59
00:06:15,160 --> 00:06:21,100
These seismic reflection surveys were conducted using Vibrasize, Primacord, and standard dynamite

60
00:06:21,200 --> 00:06:23,240
shot holes.

61
00:06:23,340 --> 00:06:29,079
The Vibrasize technique uses a short series of individual truck-mounted vibrators generating

62
00:06:29,179 --> 00:06:32,180
a controllable acoustic signal.

63
00:06:32,280 --> 00:06:37,840
Primacord and dynamite shot hole techniques generate a broadband acoustic signal.

64
00:06:37,940 --> 00:06:42,759
The Primacord source was implanted with a Ditch Witch Vibratory Plow.

65
00:06:42,859 --> 00:06:47,159
More than 100 miles of seismic reflection data have been gathered to date with more

66
00:06:47,259 --> 00:06:51,199
extensive surface coverage scheduled for future years.

67
00:06:51,299 --> 00:06:55,719
The dynamite sources were implanted in holes drilled with rotary drills.

68
00:06:55,819 --> 00:07:00,459
The data from all three types of surveys allows us to look at subsurface geologic features

69
00:07:00,559 --> 00:07:04,079
without penetrating any of the underlying horizons.

70
00:07:04,179 --> 00:07:08,779
Each of these geologic factors being studied contributes to the evaluation of potential

71
00:07:08,879 --> 00:07:10,879
repository sites.

72
00:07:10,979 --> 00:07:14,980
these data we will establish whether or not a repository can be constructed

73
00:07:15,080 --> 00:07:19,500
within these basalt layers that will isolate radioactive waste from mankind

74
00:07:19,600 --> 00:07:24,920
for as long as the waste is toxic. Hydrologic studies are examining the

75
00:07:25,020 --> 00:07:29,300
amount of water flow below the surface, the direction of the flow, the rate of

76
00:07:29,400 --> 00:07:34,399
the flow, and the isolation of one unit from another. By evaluating the data from

77
00:07:34,499 --> 00:07:38,120
these studies we can determine what areas have hydrologic properties

78
00:07:38,220 --> 00:07:43,939
favorable to the siting of a repository. The presence of groundwater near the rock

79
00:07:44,039 --> 00:07:49,160
mass comprising a repository is an important factor requiring study. Many

80
00:07:49,260 --> 00:07:52,879
basalt layers beneath the Columbia Plateau are extremely tight and should

81
00:07:52,979 --> 00:07:58,319
be reasonably devoid of groundwater. The basalts are composed of individual rock

82
00:07:58,419 --> 00:08:05,600
units layered one on top of another. Shrinkage and cooling of the original

83
00:08:05,700 --> 00:08:14,080
molten rock mass cause small fractures to form in the basalts. These fractures are easily seen

84
00:08:14,180 --> 00:08:17,600
in basalt outcrops where they have been exposed to weathering for many years.

85
00:08:18,580 --> 00:08:23,200
However, when these same fractures are buried deep beneath the land surface,

86
00:08:23,300 --> 00:08:28,080
they become very tight because of the weight of the overlying rocks,

87
00:08:28,900 --> 00:08:33,920
and also because minerals have formed in the fractures, effectively sealing them.

88
00:08:34,019 --> 00:08:39,219
extensive field studies using drill holes available throughout the Columbia Plateau

89
00:08:39,319 --> 00:08:44,699
are being used to determine a variety of hydrologic properties of individual basalt flows in the

90
00:08:44,799 --> 00:08:50,639
subsurface for example if different horizons have different water temperatures and the waters

91
00:08:50,739 --> 00:08:55,399
indicate that their age is different one can conclude that they have been isolated from one

92
00:08:55,499 --> 00:09:01,439
another many samples obtained from water bearing strata beneath the Hanford site indicate that the

93
00:09:01,540 --> 00:09:05,380
waters have been isolated from mankind for more than 20,000 years.

94
00:09:06,200 --> 00:09:11,200
This would put their initial emplacement in the subsurface at prior to or during the glacial periods.

95
00:09:12,360 --> 00:09:16,120
We have also measured the subsurface water levels at different horizons

96
00:09:16,220 --> 00:09:19,920
and determined how fast the horizons can take or yield water.

97
00:09:20,920 --> 00:09:26,000
All this gives us information on the direction and the rate of flow, as well as the extent of the flow.

98
00:09:26,880 --> 00:09:30,020
An important parameter that is being measured by these field tests

99
00:09:30,119 --> 00:09:33,399
is the hydraulic conductivity of the underlying strata.

100
00:09:34,179 --> 00:09:38,460
The hydraulic conductivity of the central portion of many deep basalt flows

101
00:09:38,560 --> 00:09:43,659
show values which are so small they are near the limit of present detection instrumentation.

102
00:09:45,279 --> 00:09:48,840
Results of our hydrologic investigations are fed to computer models

103
00:09:48,940 --> 00:09:52,039
of the groundwater flow regime of the areas being studied

104
00:09:52,139 --> 00:09:56,419
and the results used to produce a wide range of hydrologic calculations.

105
00:09:56,519 --> 00:10:02,419
This will ultimately allow us to determine sighting areas with suitable groundwater pathways.

106
00:10:02,519 --> 00:10:06,419
Studies of the interaction between nuclear waste and basalt

107
00:10:06,519 --> 00:10:10,419
are being conducted under simulated conditions in various laboratories.

108
00:10:10,519 --> 00:10:15,419
Of special importance is work at the Materials Research Center at Pennsylvania State University

109
00:10:15,519 --> 00:10:17,419
under Dr. Greg McCarthy.

110
00:10:17,519 --> 00:10:24,419
If we place our spent fuel from nuclear power generation in a basalt repository,

111
00:10:24,519 --> 00:10:31,419
there will be three barriers to the environment, three barriers protecting our nuclear wastes

112
00:10:31,519 --> 00:10:34,419
from ever getting out into the biosphere.

113
00:10:34,519 --> 00:10:37,419
The first barrier is the waste itself.

114
00:10:37,519 --> 00:10:45,419
The waste is actually little pellets of uranium dioxide with small amounts of other radioactive elements in it.

115
00:10:45,519 --> 00:10:50,419
It is encased in long, thin tubes called cladding,

116
00:10:50,519 --> 00:10:55,019
and the cladding, spent fuel, is encased in a container.

117
00:10:56,220 --> 00:11:00,360
So radionuclides, in order to get out and get to the environment,

118
00:11:00,600 --> 00:11:02,979
would first have to bridge these three barriers.

119
00:11:03,799 --> 00:11:08,219
The second barrier of protection from our radionuclides, from spent fuel,

120
00:11:08,859 --> 00:11:13,899
is an engineered structure down deep in the basalt repository

121
00:11:13,999 --> 00:11:19,939
which may consist of concrete shells or steel shells and so forth

122
00:11:20,040 --> 00:11:25,260
It may consist of special absorptive material that we purposely place in the ground.

123
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,100
And the third barrier is the basalt itself.

124
00:11:29,140 --> 00:11:38,720
In some cases, 10 or 20 miles of solid basalt in some directions, or at least 3,000 feet of basalt to the surface.

125
00:11:39,300 --> 00:11:45,860
There are two reasons why basalts appear to be very favorable rocks from a geochemical standpoint.

126
00:11:45,960 --> 00:11:59,860
The first reason is that basalts are reducing. This means that the major elements that are of concern in the spent fuel could be kept in their lower valence states.

127
00:11:59,960 --> 00:12:10,860
The second reason is that the other elements that are in spent fuel, if they should be mobilized by the water,

128
00:12:10,960 --> 00:12:17,139
Interactions between these leached radionuclides and the basalts can produce stable minerals.

129
00:12:18,100 --> 00:12:27,580
Now, in this short time, we have not discussed and we have not examined all interactions between all of the many radionuclides,

130
00:12:27,680 --> 00:12:31,659
but we have examined several, and the results look rather favorable.

131
00:12:32,239 --> 00:12:33,759
Stable minerals have been formed.

132
00:12:33,859 --> 00:12:41,439
For example, cesium-137, this is one of the radionuclides of concern in spent fuel.

133
00:12:42,139 --> 00:12:46,559
If it were to be mobilized by groundwater solutions during the thermal period

134
00:12:46,659 --> 00:12:51,480
in the presence of the basalt, what would happen if these groundwaters

135
00:12:51,580 --> 00:12:55,120
with traces of cesium-137 hit basalt?

136
00:12:55,720 --> 00:13:00,019
Well, one interaction we can confirm today from our experiments is

137
00:13:00,119 --> 00:13:08,360
If the temperature is 200 degrees or so, which is not unlikely in the immediate vicinity of the spent fuel,

138
00:13:08,899 --> 00:13:13,199
the mineral polyosite having this formula, cesium-aluminum-silicate, would form.

139
00:13:14,039 --> 00:13:18,079
The cesium comes from the mobilized radionuclide.

140
00:13:18,739 --> 00:13:21,019
The aluminum and silicon come from the basalt.

141
00:13:21,679 --> 00:13:23,319
They form polyosite.

142
00:13:24,019 --> 00:13:27,399
Polyosite is the mineral in which cesium occurs in nature.

143
00:13:28,440 --> 00:13:31,819
We know from some tests that it appears to be a very stable marrow

144
00:13:31,919 --> 00:13:37,980
and would provide excellent protection for the 3 to 600 years

145
00:13:38,080 --> 00:13:42,740
that it takes for cesium-137 to decay to other stable isotopes,

146
00:13:42,899 --> 00:13:44,659
in other words, to lose its radioactivity.

147
00:13:45,440 --> 00:13:48,980
This photograph down here shows crystals of polyosite.

148
00:13:49,080 --> 00:13:52,399
These were actually grown through a waste rock interaction

149
00:13:52,499 --> 00:13:56,899
between cesium and basalt in our laboratory.

150
00:13:57,499 --> 00:14:04,279
using equipment which allows us to simulate conditions deep within the Earth in a basalt repository.

151
00:14:05,100 --> 00:14:09,620
The waste-basalt interaction experiments are being conducted using a gold foil bag

152
00:14:09,720 --> 00:14:14,399
containing simulated spent fuel, zircaloy to represent the container of the nuclear waste,

153
00:14:15,080 --> 00:14:16,439
basalt, and groundwater.

154
00:14:17,460 --> 00:14:21,799
These gold foil bags are then emplaced in a high-temperature, high-pressure chamber

155
00:14:21,899 --> 00:14:25,759
where the experiments are conducted under simulated repository conditions.

156
00:14:26,700 --> 00:14:30,639
Rock mechanics is a study of the behavior of rock under various conditions.

157
00:14:31,580 --> 00:14:35,819
In the waste isolation project, the rock mechanics effort has several objectives.

158
00:14:36,759 --> 00:14:43,939
The first is to determine the thermal and mechanical properties pertinent to the design and construction of the repository in basalt.

159
00:14:44,999 --> 00:14:53,980
Next, we modify and or develop computer programs needed during repository design to simulate the thermal and mechanical behavior of the rock mass.

160
00:14:54,080 --> 00:15:01,879
We must formulate a structural design of the repository and provide needed input for the design and construction phases.

161
00:15:03,180 --> 00:15:10,500
Engineering data is being obtained through laboratory studies of the thermal and mechanical properties of the rock core at the Colorado School of Mines.

162
00:15:11,359 --> 00:15:19,680
In mechanical properties, we're testing the strength of the rock and the deformation property of the rock,

163
00:15:19,780 --> 00:15:37,160
the sonic velocity, density, porosity, and in the thermal properties, we're testing the thermal expansion, conductivity, and also the specific heat.

164
00:15:38,700 --> 00:15:43,580
The thermal property is used to determine the thermal effect of spent fuel on basalt.

165
00:15:44,500 --> 00:15:50,360
As to date, the Colorado School of Mines findings indicate that basalt is a very strong rock

166
00:15:50,460 --> 00:15:57,060
with a compressive strength of 30,000 to 40,000 psi and deforms very little under high load.

167
00:15:57,940 --> 00:16:00,580
These are favorable properties for nuclear waste storage.

168
00:16:01,859 --> 00:16:09,300
Data from these rock property studies will be used in models to analyze rock behavior under natural and induced stresses.

169
00:16:09,400 --> 00:16:16,760
such modeling studies will permit an analysis and are necessary to develop alternative designs for a repository.

170
00:16:18,540 --> 00:16:23,720
A computer model basically combines the rock properties, the site conditions,

171
00:16:24,340 --> 00:16:28,280
the underground excavation design, and the heat properties of the nuclear fuel

172
00:16:28,380 --> 00:16:33,720
and determines the distributions of temperatures and stress in the rock mass surrounding the repository.

173
00:16:33,820 --> 00:16:39,680
To examine how the basalt reacts to heat stresses from the emplacement of nuclear waste,

174
00:16:40,280 --> 00:16:44,920
a near-surface test facility has been developed as a full-scale laboratory demonstration.

175
00:16:45,879 --> 00:16:49,500
This facility is located on the Hanford site near Richland, Washington.

176
00:16:50,460 --> 00:16:54,920
This artist sketch illustrates the layout of the near-surface test facility.

177
00:16:55,619 --> 00:16:57,940
This facility is being developed in two phases.

178
00:16:58,739 --> 00:17:02,600
Phase one houses a series of electric heater tests.

179
00:17:02,700 --> 00:17:10,599
These tests will simulate the thermal effects of radioactive material in an underground basalt environment.

180
00:17:10,699 --> 00:17:16,599
Phase II will house a series of tests with actual radioactive material.

181
00:17:16,699 --> 00:17:21,599
The near-surface test facility is located on the north side of Gable Mountain.

182
00:17:21,699 --> 00:17:29,599
This excavation is for the east portal, providing access to the extensometer room and the phase I test room.

183
00:17:29,699 --> 00:17:35,139
The center excavation, called the west portal, provides the main access to the electric heater

184
00:17:35,239 --> 00:17:37,159
area.

185
00:17:37,259 --> 00:17:42,219
The excavation on the right is the main access to the phase two testing.

186
00:17:42,319 --> 00:17:48,159
The phase one electric heater test program began in July of 1980 with the phase two radioactive

187
00:17:48,259 --> 00:17:52,779
material program scheduled to begin in 1982.

188
00:17:52,879 --> 00:17:57,679
The testing takes place in a basalt layer called the Pomona flow.

189
00:17:57,780 --> 00:18:01,920
about the Pomona flow was collected from outcrops and two geologic test holes

190
00:18:02,020 --> 00:18:07,460
drilled on the north side of Gable Mountain. Laboratory tests indicate that

191
00:18:07,560 --> 00:18:11,360
the Pomona flow is similar to the flows encountered at depths which are being

192
00:18:11,460 --> 00:18:16,560
considered as potential sites for a repository. In addition to the near

193
00:18:16,660 --> 00:18:21,320
surface test facility, much actual mining information was obtained from the Bacon

194
00:18:21,420 --> 00:18:26,779
siphon and tunnel project number one, completed in 1948, and the Bacon siphon

195
00:18:26,879 --> 00:18:29,980
in tunnel number two, recently completed near Coulee City,

196
00:18:30,080 --> 00:18:31,759
Washington.

197
00:18:31,859 --> 00:18:37,899
Project number two is of particular significance to us, not as an irrigation project, but rather as a major

198
00:18:37,999 --> 00:18:40,879
underground excavation in basalt.

199
00:18:40,979 --> 00:18:49,220
This tunnel has a finished inside diameter of 28 and 1 half feet, and is 9,950 feet long.

200
00:18:49,320 --> 00:18:52,819
Much valuable information of a geologic and engineering nature

201
00:18:52,919 --> 00:18:56,180
is available from projects such as this.

202
00:18:56,280 --> 00:19:03,740
The cycle of operations, drilling, blasting, and mucking used so successfully at the Bacon Siphon and Tunnel Project No. 2

203
00:19:03,840 --> 00:19:10,360
are similar to those which were used at the near-surface test facility and projected for the waste isolation repository.

204
00:19:11,240 --> 00:19:16,360
Heater startup at the near-surface test facility began on schedule, July 1, 1980.

205
00:19:17,160 --> 00:19:24,820
In one test, a single 5-kilowatt heater is used to measure the ability of the basalt to dissipate the heat from a single waste canister.

206
00:19:25,479 --> 00:19:32,980
The second test uses a 5-kilowatt heater surrounded by a circular array of eight smaller 1-kilowatt

207
00:19:33,080 --> 00:19:37,819
heaters to simulate the thermal effects of multiple canister disposal in basalt.

208
00:19:39,219 --> 00:19:46,939
Here, in the extensometer room, which measures 21 feet high, 14 feet wide, and 164 feet long,

209
00:19:47,359 --> 00:19:52,439
and runs parallel to the heater test room, workers install the extensometers and gauges

210
00:19:52,540 --> 00:19:58,059
that will provide data on expansion, stress, and temperature changes within the basalt mass.

211
00:19:58,680 --> 00:20:03,559
In addition to using extensometers, U.S. Bureau of Mines and IRAD gauges

212
00:20:03,659 --> 00:20:05,940
are used in the measurement of stress and tension.

213
00:20:07,820 --> 00:20:13,180
The data from the instrumentation surrounding the heaters is relayed to data loggers and recorders

214
00:20:13,280 --> 00:20:17,140
in the instrument enclosure area adjacent to the heater test area.

215
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,820
The information is retained on magnetic tape cartridges.

216
00:20:20,919 --> 00:20:25,960
in addition to being transferred to a central computer system located outside the west portal.

217
00:20:27,019 --> 00:20:31,899
There, a team of computer specialists convert the data for use by the scientists and engineers

218
00:20:31,999 --> 00:20:35,319
who are studying the thermal and mechanical properties of basalt.

219
00:20:36,999 --> 00:20:40,519
Initial heater startup power was set at 250 watts,

220
00:20:41,060 --> 00:20:44,319
culminating more than two and one half years of planning and construction.

221
00:20:45,340 --> 00:20:49,759
The power levels will be increased by steps up to the maximum of one kilowatt

222
00:20:49,859 --> 00:20:52,960
for both the main heaters and peripheral heaters.

223
00:20:53,320 --> 00:20:57,059
A future series of tests commencing in early 1982

224
00:20:57,159 --> 00:21:00,259
use two canisters of radioactive spent fuel

225
00:21:00,359 --> 00:21:03,660
and one canister of vitrified commercial high-level waste.

226
00:21:04,679 --> 00:21:08,019
These tests will be conducted to demonstrate and develop technology

227
00:21:08,119 --> 00:21:11,279
as well as equipment for handling spent fuel in basalt.

228
00:21:12,659 --> 00:21:19,599
It will also provide scientists and engineers the opportunity to demonstrate the placement, storage, and retrieval of spent fuel

229
00:21:19,700 --> 00:21:22,380
while showing the effects of radiation on the basalt.

230
00:21:23,340 --> 00:21:31,060
Results from the studies at the Near Surface Test Facility are of major importance in assessing the feasibility of siting a repository in basalt.

231
00:21:31,820 --> 00:21:34,960
At the conclusion of NSTF testing in the mid-1980s,

232
00:21:35,060 --> 00:21:38,620
the site will be decommissioned and returned to its natural state.

233
00:21:42,900 --> 00:21:48,340
You have seen a glimpse of some of the work that is presently underway at Rockville Panther

234
00:21:48,439 --> 00:21:54,099
and with some of our contractors to look at the feasibility of storing nuclear waste in Basalt.

235
00:21:54,919 --> 00:22:00,359
The results of all these investigations are being reported to the scientific community and the public

236
00:22:00,459 --> 00:22:02,980
and many results are already available.

237
00:22:04,179 --> 00:22:10,259
During this year alone, over 100 reports have been issued to cover the results of all these investigations.

238
00:22:11,439 --> 00:22:17,159
In addition, I'm standing here at the data library where all this information eventually comes together

239
00:22:17,260 --> 00:22:21,040
and where the information is used by the scientists and engineers

240
00:22:21,140 --> 00:22:25,400
that are going to be responsible for making that final decision.

241
00:22:26,300 --> 00:22:30,160
Is it feasible to store nuclear waste in basalt or not?

242
00:22:30,700 --> 00:22:37,779
At this point in time, we have moved very far toward the point where we can make that decision.

243
00:22:38,560 --> 00:22:44,380
But there is still some technological achievements to be accomplished in the next few years.

244
00:22:44,480 --> 00:22:52,420
Much data has been gathered. For example, as we showed you today, a large amount of

245
00:22:52,520 --> 00:22:58,940
holes have been drilled at Hanford. And the data from these holes is recorded in logbooks

246
00:22:59,040 --> 00:23:05,600
which we store here. Much data in the geologic investigations is already available. And much

247
00:23:05,700 --> 00:23:13,160
more data will be available in the next few years. We expect that a decision on the feasibility

248
00:23:13,260 --> 00:23:18,120
storing nuclear waste in basalt will be made in the forthcoming years.
